.\" Manpage for sshub.
.\" Contributions and corrections welcome at https://github.com/Petyok/SSHub
.TH SSHUB 1 "2026-07-18" "sshub 0.9.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sshub \- keyboard-driven TUI SSH host launcher with a headless CLI
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sshub
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.br
.B sshub
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARGS ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sshub
is a terminal UI for managing and connecting to SSH hosts. It merges the
read-only
.I ~/.ssh/config
with a launcher-managed SQLite database and provides nested groups, fuzzy
search, tags, embedded PTY sessions, SFTP transfers, SSH tunnels, identity
management, and an audit log.
.PP
Run with no command to launch the TUI. Any recognized
.I COMMAND
runs headless (no TUI) and is scriptable: output honors
.B \-\-format
.RB ( plain
or
.BR json )
where applicable, and the exit status follows the convention in
.BR "EXIT STATUS" .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help. After a command
.RB ( "sshub " "\fICOMMAND\fR \fB\-\-help\fR" )
it prints help for that command.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Exit immediately without launching the TUI (smoke tests / CI).
.TP
.B \-\-profile \fINAME\fR
Use the named profile. For the TUI this skips the startup picker; for a
headless command it selects the profile instead of the last-used one. Unknown
names fail with the list of available profiles. Rejected in compatibility mode
(see
.BR ENVIRONMENT ).
.TP
.B \-\-manage\-profiles
Open the profile picker at startup even when only one profile exists.
.PP
Both flags may appear before or after a command; anything after
.B \-\-
belongs to the remote command and is never read as a profile flag.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Host (read)
.TP
.B sshub host list \fR[\fB\-\-tag\fR \fITAG\fR]... [\fB\-\-group\fR \fINAME\fR] [\fB\-\-sort\fR \fIMODE\fR] [\fB\-\-format\fR \fIF\fR]
List hosts.
.B \-\-tag
is repeatable and ANDs; \fB\-\-group\fR filters by exact membership (not the
subtree). \fIMODE\fR is one of
.BR label ", " last\-connected ", " favorite ", " group ", " manual .
.TP
.B sshub host show \fINAME\fR [\fB\-\-format\fR \fIF\fR]
Show the full fact sheet for a host.
.TP
.B sshub host connect \fINAME\fR \fR[\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR]
Connect over SSH (or mosh, per the host transport). Session logging and audit
apply as configured. The child exit code is propagated.
.TP
.B sshub host resolve \fINAME\fR [\fB\-\-format\fR \fIF\fR]
Print the resolved connection details and the exact
.I argv
that would run, plus whether a stored secret exists (never the secret itself).
.TP
.B sshub host search \fIQUERY\fR [\fB\-\-format\fR \fIF\fR]
Fuzzy-search hosts, best match first.
.SS Host (write)
.TP
.B sshub host add \fB\-\-name\fR \fINAME\fR \fB\-\-address\fR \fIADDR\fR [flags]
Create a launcher host. Flags include
.BR \-\-port ", " \-\-username ", " \-\-label ", " \-\-identity ", " \-\-group " (repeatable), "
.BR \-\-tags " (comma-separated), " \-\-notes ", " \-\-proxy\-jump ", " \-\-remote\-command ", "
.BR \-\-forward\-agent ", " \-\-no\-forward\-agent ", " \-\-os\-icon ", " \-\-transport ", "
.BR \-\-session\-log ", " \-\-ping ", " \-\-favorite ", " \-\-password\-stdin ", " \-\-secret\-lookup .
.B \-\-secret\-lookup \fIATTR\fB=\fIVALUE\fR
takes the host password from the one Secret Service entry (e.g. KeePassXC)
whose attribute matches exactly, such as
.B URL=ssh://user@host:22
or
.BR Uuid=... ;
edit takes
.BR \-\-set\-secret\-lookup " and " \-\-clear\-secret\-lookup .
.TP
.B sshub host edit \fB\-\-name\fR \fINAME\fR [\fB\-\-set\-*\fR|\fB\-\-clear\-*\fR ...]
Edit a host. Each field has a
.BI \-\-set\- FIELD
and, where nullable, a
.BI \-\-clear\- FIELD
form (e.g.
.BR \-\-set\-username ", " \-\-clear\-username ", " \-\-set\-os\-icon ,
.BR \-\-set\-tags ", " \-\-set\-group ", " \-\-set\-identity ", " \-\-set\-transport ).
.TP
.B sshub host rename \fB\-\-name\fR \fINAME\fR \fB\-\-new\-name\fR \fINEW\fR [\fB\-\-strict\fR]
Rename a launcher host.
.B \-\-strict
fails instead of de-duplicating a clashing name.
.TP
.B sshub host delete \fB\-\-name\fR \fINAME\fR \fB\-\-yes\fR
Delete a launcher host. Requires
.BR \-\-yes .
.TP
.B sshub host duplicate \fB\-\-name\fR \fINAME\fR [\fB\-\-new\-name\fR \fINEW\fR] [\fB\-\-format\fR \fIF\fR]
Duplicate a host (legacy ssh_config hosts are copied into the launcher).
Without
.BR \-\-new\-name ,
the copy gets a free name derived from the original.
.TP
.B sshub host transfer \fB\-\-name\fR \fINAME\fR \fB\-\-to\fR \fIPROFILE\fR \fR[\fB\-\-move\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR]
Copy a launcher host into another profile. The plan is printed first and
applied only after an interactive
.B y
or with
.BR \-\-yes ;
.B \-\-move
deletes the original afterwards and carries the saved password of a moved
host (and the passphrase of a moved identity) into the destination profile
first; a secret that cannot be carried keeps its row in the source, is
reported on stderr, and makes the command exit 1. A copy carries no secrets.
The group, identity and tunnel
.B transfer
commands below work the same way.
.SS Exec (scripted)
.TP
.B sshub exec \fIHOST\fR \fR[\fB\-\-tty\fR] [\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fISECS\fR] [\fB\-\-format\fR \fIF\fR] [\fB\-v\fR] \fB\-\-\fR \fICOMMAND\fR \fR[\fIARGS\fR...]
Run one command on a saved host and exit with the remote command's exit code
(ssh's own failures keep ssh's 255). stdout and stderr pass through, stdin is
inherited so pipes work, and nothing ever prompts \(em with no stored credential
exec runs ssh in
.BR BatchMode ,
so an unknown host key fails instead of waiting for a human. Everything after
.B \-\-
is the command; a single quoted string
.RB ( "sshub exec web-01 \(dquptime\(dq" )
works too. Shell operators are read by whichever shell sees them first:
.RB ( "sshub exec web-01 \-\- ls && uptime" )
runs
.B ls
on the host and
.B uptime
at home, exactly as
.BR ssh (1)
does \(em quote the whole command
.RB ( "sshub exec web-01 \-\- \(dqls && uptime\(dq" )
to send it all remotely. A full-screen command (vim, top, less) needs
.BR \-\-tty .
.B \-\-tty
forces a PTY
.RB ( "ssh \-tt" )
for commands that insist on one; without it exec runs
.BR "ssh \-T" .
.B \-\-timeout
kills the command after
.I SECS
and exits 124, like
.BR timeout (1);
the run is spawned into its own process group and the group is killed, so a
.B ProxyJump
helper cannot outlive it (with a timeout,
.B Ctrl-C
therefore no longer reaches ssh).
.B \-\-format
.B json
buffers the run as
.IR {host, " " command, " " exit_code, " " stdout, " " stderr, " " duration_ms} ;
plain (the default) streams. A remote command stored on the host or in
.I ~/.ssh/config
is overridden by the command given here. Session transcripts are skipped for exec \(em
.BR script (1)
wrapping fights output redirection. Mosh hosts are refused: mosh has no one-shot
command mode. Runs are audited as
.BR "via exec" .
.SS Aliases
.TP
.B sshub connect \fINAME\fR
Alias for
.BR "host connect" .
.TP
.B sshub list \fR...
Alias for
.BR "host list" .
.TP
.B sshub groups \fR...
Alias for
.BR "group list" " (forwards flags, e.g. " \-\-all ).
.SS Groups
.TP
.B sshub group list \fR[\fB\-\-all\fR] [\fB\-\-format\fR \fIF\fR]
.TQ
.B sshub group show \fINAME\fR [\fB\-\-format\fR \fIF\fR]
.TQ
.B sshub group add \fB\-\-name\fR \fINAME\fR \fR[\fB\-\-parent\fR \fIG\fR] [\fB\-\-default\-identity\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-sort\-order\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-default\-\fIFIELD\fR ...]
.TQ
.B sshub group edit \fB\-\-name\fR \fINAME\fR \fR[\fB\-\-set\-name\fR|\fB\-\-set\-parent\fR|\fB\-\-clear\-parent\fR|\fB\-\-set\-default\-identity\fR|\fB\-\-clear\-default\-identity\fR|\fB\-\-set\-sort\-order\fR|\fB\-\-set\-default\-\fIFIELD\fR|\fB\-\-clear\-default\-\fIFIELD\fR ...]
.TQ
.B sshub group delete \fB\-\-name\fR \fINAME\fR \fB\-\-yes\fR
.TQ
.B sshub group transfer \fB\-\-name\fR \fINAME\fR \fB\-\-to\fR \fIPROFILE\fR \fR[\fB\-\-with\-hosts\fR] [\fB\-\-move\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR]
Manage host groups.
.I FIELD
is a connection default a member host inherits when it leaves that field empty:
.BR username ", " port ", " proxy\-jump ", " transport " (" ssh | mosh ),
and
.BR fwd\-agent .
On add, give
.BI \-\-default\- FIELD " " VALUE
.RB ( \-\-default\-fwd\-agent
or
.B \-\-no\-default\-fwd\-agent
take no value); on edit,
.BI \-\-set\-default\- FIELD
or
.BI \-\-clear\-default\- FIELD
.RB ( \-\-set\-default\-fwd\-agent
and
.B \-\-no\-default\-fwd\-agent
take no value). Per field a host value wins, then the nearest group default,
then the global default (port 22, ssh, forwarding off).
.B transfer \-\-with\-hosts
also transfers the group's member hosts; with
.B \-\-move
the moved hosts' saved passwords go along, as with
.BR "host transfer" .
.SS Identities
.TP
.B sshub identity list\fR|\fBshow\fR|\fBadd\fR|\fBedit\fR|\fBdelete\fR|\fBagent\-remove\fR|\fBtransfer \fR...
Manage SSH identities. Passwords are read from stdin with
.BR \-\-password\-stdin .
.B agent\-remove \-\-name \fINAME\fR
runs
.B ssh\-add \-d
for the identity's key.
.B transfer \-\-name \fINAME\fB \-\-to \fIPROFILE\fR
copies the key path but no secret; with
.B \-\-move
the saved passphrase is carried to the destination profile.
.SS Tunnels
.TP
.B sshub tunnel list\fR|\fBshow \fR[\fB\-\-format\fR \fIF\fR]
.TQ
.B sshub tunnel create \fB\-\-host\fR \fINAME\fR \fB\-\-type\fR \fIT\fR \fB\-\-local\-port\fR \fIP\fR \fR[\fB\-\-remote\-host\fR \fIH\fR] [\fB\-\-remote\-port\fR \fIP\fR] [\fB\-\-label\fR \fIL\fR] [\fB\-\-keep\-alive\fR]
.TQ
.B sshub tunnel start \fIID\fR \fR[\fB\-\-foreground\fR]
.TQ
.B sshub tunnel stop \fIID\fR
.TQ
.B sshub tunnel delete \fIID\fR \fB\-\-yes\fR
.TQ
.B sshub tunnel transfer \fIID\fR \fB\-\-to\fR \fIPROFILE\fR \fR[\fB\-\-move\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR]
Manage SSH tunnels.
.I T
is
.BR local ", " remote ", or " dynamic .
By default
.B start
detaches and records a PID file under the data directory;
.B \-\-foreground
runs in-process with the reconnect loop.
.IP
Note: CLI-detached tunnels (PID file) and the TUI's in-process tunnel manager
are mutually invisible.
.SS SFTP (one-shot)
.TP
.B sshub sftp ls \fIHOST\fR \fR[\fIPATH\fR] [\fB\-\-format\fR \fIF\fR]
.TQ
.B sshub sftp get \fIHOST\fR \fIREMOTE\fR \fR[\fILOCAL\fR] [\fB\-\-recursive\fR]
.TQ
.B sshub sftp put \fIHOST\fR \fILOCAL\fR \fR[\fIREMOTE\fR] [\fB\-\-recursive\fR]
.TQ
.B sshub sftp rm \fIHOST\fR \fIREMOTE\fR \fR[\fB\-\-recursive\fR] \fB\-\-yes\fR
.TQ
.B sshub sftp mkdir\fR|\fBrename\fR|\fBchmod \fR...
One-shot SFTP over a direct host (ProxyJump is not supported).
.B chmod
takes an octal mode.
.B rm
is destructive and requires
.BR \-\-yes .
.SS Audit
.TP
.B sshub audit list \fR[\fB\-\-status\fR \fIS\fR] [\fB\-\-via\fR \fIV\fR] [\fB\-\-host\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-limit\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-days\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-format\fR \fIF\fR]
.TQ
.B sshub audit stats \fR[\fB\-\-days\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-via\fR \fIV\fR] [\fB\-\-include\-retry\fR] [\fB\-\-format\fR \fIF\fR]
Inspect the connection audit log.
.I S
is
.BR all ", " ok ", " fail ", or " retry ;
.I V
is
.BR all ", " connect ", " tunnel ", " agent ", " exec ", or " broadcast .
.SS Map
.TP
.B sshub map \-\-dot
Print the jump-host map as a Graphviz
.B digraph
(own ProxyJump, group defaults and ssh_config jumps only; jump loops red, a
ProxyCommand as one opaque
.B proxy cmd
box). Pipe it to
.BR dot (1).
.SS Themes
.TP
.B sshub theme list \fR[\fB\-\-format\fR \fBplain\fR|\fBjson\fR]
List every built-in and user theme with its display name, source and validation
state. Returns
.B 0
whenever the registry could be read, even if individual themes are
.BR warning " or " invalid .
.TP
.B sshub theme show \fIID\fR \fR[\fB\-\-resolved\fR] [\fB\-\-format\fR \fBtoml\fR|\fBjson\fR]
Print a theme. Without
.B \-\-resolved
this is the file's own source, comments included, which is what makes the copy
workflow work. With
.B \-\-resolved
it is a complete standalone document with every semantic slot, gradient and
component role written out and no
.IR extends ;
it re-reads to the same runtime theme. An unknown
.I ID
exits
.BR 1 .
.TP
.B sshub theme check \fIFILE\fR \fR[\fB\-\-format\fR \fBplain\fR|\fBjson\fR]
Validate a theme file in strict mode with the same parser and resolver the
application uses. Diagnostics carry file, line and column where the TOML parser
supplies a position, and suggest a near match for an unknown key. Independent
problems are collected in one run. Exits
.B 0
when valid (warnings allowed) and
.B 1
on any error.
.PP
All three run headless: no TUI, no database, no keyring. User themes live in
the selected profile's
.I themes/*.toml
(see
.BR FILES )
and the file name is the theme ID.
.B theme check
takes any file path. The selected theme is stored as
.I appearance.active_theme
in the profile's
.IR config.toml .
Select a theme in the TUI with
.BR Ctrl+H " \(-> " Theme... " \(-> " Enter :
moving through the list previews live, Esc rolls back, Enter saves.
.PP
The full guide (file format, colour values and simulated opacity,
inheritance and
.IR \(dqauto\(dq ,
gradient directions and the perimeter rule, the complete role catalogue, and
worked examples) is in
.I docs/theme\-system.md
in the source tree.
.SS Host sync (headless, encrypted peer-to-peer)
.TP
.B sshub sync\-join \fIHOST\fR
Join the ring of the device at
.IR HOST ,
a launcher host name or
.RI [ USER @] HOST [: PORT ].
Runs
.B sshub sync\-enroll
there over ssh. The four SAS words and the peer name are printed on stdout.
If the other end has a screen too, compare the words and answer
.B y
on stdin only if they are identical (anything else, or EOF, refuses). If it has
none, the words prove nothing: the command says so and asks
.B Admit this device?
instead; the device's identity then rests on the SSH host key you verified. The
success line names the role (joined a ring, or admitted the peer) and how the
device was verified (by SAS, or by the SSH host key only). Exits 0 when joined, 1 on failure,
2 on usage error.
.TP
.B sshub sync\-now \fR[\fIDEVICE\fR] [\fB\-\-format\fR \fBplain\fR|\fBjson\fR]
Sync with one ring device (name or unique id prefix) or, without
.IR DEVICE ,
with every other ring device that has a known host; the rest are reported as
.BR "skipped (no host known)"
(with how to record one: the hosts tab key
.BR t ,
or
.BR "sshub sync\-join" )
and a device still waiting for approval as
.BR "not admitted yet" .
One line per device:
.B <name>: ok applied N, pending M, received R, sent S
(plus
.B behind
and
.B suspect
ids). Exit 0 on success or when nothing could be attempted, 1 when a device was
offline or failed, 2 on usage error, 3 when a fork is proven
.RB ( "FORK DETECTED: <name>" ).
.TP
.B sshub sync\-status \fR[\fB\-\-format\fR \fBplain\fR|\fBjson\fR]
Read-only: ring devices (name, short id, role, status, last seen, head seq,
host), pending change count and credential mode. Never creates a device key.
.TP
.B sshub sync\-review \fR[\fB\-\-approve\fR \fIID\fR | \fB\-\-reject\fR \fIID\fR] [\fB\-\-format\fR \fBplain\fR|\fBjson\fR]
List the changes waiting for review: a 12-character id, the author device, a
summary and one line per field (marked
.B [!]
when dangerous). Every waiting change is listed with the reason it waits
(in brackets; the
.B reason
field of the JSON items) and a Red change with the proofs of its ceremony. With
.B \-\-approve
or
.B \-\-reject
the change whose id starts with
.I ID
(at least 4 hex characters, unique) is resolved in the store like in the TUI.
Never creates a device key. Exit 0 on success or empty queue, 1 on a store
error, 2 on an unknown or ambiguous id or bad options.
.TP
.B sshub sync\-serve
.TQ
.B sshub sync\-enroll
Run by a peer over ssh; stdin and stdout carry protocol frames only (one sync
round, or the existing-device side of an enrollment). Not for interactive use.
Enrollment roles come from the handshake: the side that already has a ring
invites, a blank side joins; two different rings are refused.
.B sync\-enroll
asks for the SAS confirmation on /dev/tty only when stdin is a terminal
.RB ( "ssh \-t" ).
Otherwise there is no human on that side: it never confirms as one, tells the
peer it has no screen, admits on the strength of the authenticated ssh session
and prints
.B SAS words: <words> (peer has screen: ...)
to stderr for the log only.
Exit 0 on success, 1 on error.
.SS Inventory and config
.TP
.B sshub tags \fR[\fB\-\-format\fR \fIF\fR]
List every tag in the inventory.
.TP
.B sshub sync
Refresh the ssh_config-derived rows in the database.
.TP
.B sshub import \fR[\fB\-\-from\fR \fBssh\fR|\fBtermius\fR|\fBputty\fR|\fBmremoteng\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] \fR[\fIPATH\fR]
Import hosts into the launcher from an external source.
.B \-\-from
selects the format (default
.BR ssh ):
.B ssh
reads
.IR ~/.ssh/config ,
.B termius
reads a Termius export directory (containing
.IR L00t.csv ),
.B putty
reads a Windows regedit
.I .reg
export file or a Unix
.I ~/.putty/sessions
directory (the default when
.I PATH
is omitted), and
.B mremoteng
reads an mRemoteNG
.I confCons.xml
file.
.I PATH
is required for
.BR mremoteng ,
optional for
.B termius
and
.B putty
(each has a default location), and ignored for
.BR ssh .
.B \-\-dry\-run
parses and previews the hosts without writing them (not supported for
.BR ssh ).
Only SSH sessions are imported (non-SSH entries such as RDP, VNC, or telnet are
skipped), and encrypted mRemoteNG passwords are not decrypted, so imported hosts
carry no stored secret.
.TP
.B sshub export \fR[\fB\-\-stdout\fR | \fB\-o\fR \fIPATH\fR]
Export launcher hosts to an ssh_config snippet.
.B \-\-stdout
and
.B \-o
are mutually exclusive.
.TP
.B sshub completions \fBbash\fR|\fBzsh\fR|\fBfish\fR \fR[\fB\-\-cache\fR \fIPATH\fR]
Print a shell completion script.
.B \-\-cache
writes/reads a host-name cache for faster dynamic completion.
.TP
.B sshub db purge \fR[\fB\-\-yes\-i\-am\-stupid\fR]
Delete the selected profile's launcher database (hosts, groups, identities,
tunnels, audit log); keyring passwords are left in place.
Irreversible; requires
.BR \-\-yes\-i\-am\-stupid .
Leaves
.I ~/.ssh/config
untouched.
.TP
.B sshub sync\-revoke \fIDEVICE\fR
Revoke a device: Yellow for a mirror, Red for a holder. Prints the ceremony status.
.TP
.B sshub sync\-role \fIDEVICE\fR \fBholder\fR|\fBmirror\fR
Promote or demote a device (Red).
.TP
.B sshub sync\-approve \fIOP\fR
Approve a pending Red op as this attended device.
.TP
.B sshub sync\-factor \fIOP\fR \fB\-\-paper\fR|\fB\-\-email\-code\fR
Add the third factor to a Red op; the secret is read without echo.
.TP
.B sshub sync\-factor\-register \fBpaper\fR|\fBemail\fR \fR[\fB\-\-sender\fR \fIDEVICE\fR]
Register a third factor. The emailed code is kept, sealed, only on its sender: register it on
the device that will mail it (\fB\-\-sender\fR may only name this device).
.TP
.B sshub sync\-phone\-enroll
Enroll the phone from this terminal as text. Prints the terminal's frames; read the phone's frames
(base64, one per line) and then \fBy\fR or \fBn\fR for the four words from standard input. A verified phone is
registered and its admission proposed (a Red change).
.TP
.B sshub sync\-notice \fIOP\fR
Print the kind 3 notice (base64) of a Red op for the phone.
.TP
.B sshub sync\-phone\-ops
Take in the ops the phone copied (kind 5, base64) from standard input and print what applied.
.TP
.B sshub sync\-share\-request \fIHOST\fR \fR[\fB\-\-wait\fR \fISECS\fR]
Print the kind 1 challenge for the phone, read its kind 2 reply from standard input and open the credential
in this process.
.TP
.B sshub sync\-cert\-request \fR(\fB\-\-group\fR \fINAME\fR|\fB\-\-host\fR \fINAME\fR...) [\fB\-\-days\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-budget\fR \fIN\fR]
Print the kind 9 trust-session request for the phone. Every verb takes \fB\-\-format\fR \fBplain\fR|\fBjson\fR.
.TP
.B sshub sync\-recovery \fBstart\fR \fR[\fB\-\-paper\fR]|\fBapprove\fR \fIOP\fR|\fBack\fR \fIOP\fR|\fBcancel\fR \fIOP\fR \fB\-\-factor\fR \fBpaper\fR|\fBemail\fR|\fBstatus\fR
Phone re-enrollment.
.TP
.B sshub sync\-mode \fBvault\fR|\fBquorum\fR \fR[\fB\-\-cosigner\fR \fIDEVICE\fR]
Change the credential mode (Red), in two steps: the first call proposes the change
and shows the ceremony, a later call (after the ring agreed) re-encrypts the credentials.
.TP
.B sshub sync\-exemption \fBon\fR|\fBoff\fR \fIDEVICE\fR
Master-phrase exemption of a device.
.TP
.B sshub sync\-export \fB\-\-window\fR \fISECS\fR \fB\-\-out\fR \fIPATH\fR \fR[\fB\-\-wait\fR \fISECS\fR]
Bulk export inside a Red-approved window, as a Termius CSV (mode 0600).
.TP
.B sshub sync\-migrate \fR[\fB\-\-remove\-local\fR]
Write existing local secrets as replicated credentials.
.TP
.B sshub sync\-import\-kit
Read the ring kit and the six master words without echo.
.PP
All of these accept
.B \-\-format plain|json
and the global
.BR \-\-profile .
.PP
The phone's sound and webcam receivers are opt-in cargo features, off in
release binaries:
.B audio
needs the ALSA development files on Linux (libasound2\-dev on Debian and
Ubuntu) and uses CoreAudio on macOS;
.B camera
needs nothing extra on Linux (V4L2) and uses AVFoundation on macOS;
.B bluetooth
(Bluetooth Low Energy, the phone page connects with Web Bluetooth) needs a running
bluetoothd and an adapter on Linux (libdbus\-1\-dev to build) and uses CoreBluetooth on
macOS. The phone verbs take
.B \-\-ble
to carry their frames over Bluetooth too. Developer check:
.B cargo build \-\-features audio,camera,bluetooth
and
.BR "cargo clippy \-\-all\-targets \-\-features audio,camera,bluetooth \-\- \-D warnings" .
Design and threat model:
.I docs/host\-sync\-design.md
in the source tree.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Operational failure (not found, a destructive command refused without
.BR \-\-yes ,
spawn error, non-zero ssh exit, hard import error).
.TP
.B 124
.B sshub exec \-\-timeout
killed the remote command.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error, bad flags, deleting a non-launcher host, or deleting an identity
still in use.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SSHUB_CONFIG_DIR
Override the config directory (fallback:
.BR SSH_LAUNCHER_CONFIG_DIR ).
.TP
.B SSHUB_DATA_DIR
Override the data directory (fallback:
.BR SSH_LAUNCHER_DATA_DIR ).
.TP
.B SSHUB_SSH_CONFIG
Override the SSH config path (fallback:
.BR SSH_LAUNCHER_SSH_CONFIG ).
.TP
.B SSHUB_DRY_RUN
Exit immediately (fallback:
.BR SSH_LAUNCHER_DRY_RUN ).
.TP
.B SSHUB_AUTO_QUIT
Headless smoke test (fallback:
.BR SSH_LAUNCHER_AUTO_QUIT ):
.B q
quits through the
.B q
key, any other value quits after the first draw. Also skips the startup picker
and the update check.
.TP
.B SSHUB_VERSION_LABEL
Replace the version label in the tab bar; set but empty hides it.
.TP
.B SSHUB_UNLOCK_WAIT
Seconds a command waits, in Quorum mode, for an attended holder to approve its share
release (default 120; 0 does not ask). The holder approves in its TUI or with
.BR "sshub sync\-review \-\-approve" .
.TP
.B SSHUB_INSTALL_CHANNEL
How sshub was installed
.RB ( npm ", " cargo ", " source ,
or another name); decides the route the in-app update takes and the suffix
next to the version. The npm wrapper sets
.BR npm .
.PP
Setting
.B SSHUB_CONFIG_DIR
or
.B SSHUB_DATA_DIR
selects compatibility mode: the directories are used verbatim, profiles are
not discovered, and
.B \-\-profile
is rejected.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/sshub/state.toml
The profile registry and the last-used profile.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/sshub/profiles/NAME/
One profile:
.I launcher.db
(managed hosts, groups, identities, tunnels, audit log),
.IR metadata.db ,
.IR config.toml ,
.IR themes/*.toml ,
session
.IR logs/ ,
tunnel
.IR tunnels/ ,
and
.I credentials.json
when no Secret Service keyring is available. In compatibility mode the
databases, logs and tunnels sit directly in
.B SSHUB_DATA_DIR
(default
.IR ~/.local/share/sshub )
and
.I config.toml
and
.I themes/
in
.B SSHUB_CONFIG_DIR
(default
.IR ~/.config/sshub ).
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/sshub/profiles/NAME/themes/*.toml
User theme files of profile NAME; the file name without
.I .toml
is the theme ID.
.B theme list
and
.B theme show
read the profile given by
.BR \-\-profile ,
else the last used one. In compatibility mode the directory is
.I ~/.config/sshub/themes
(honors
.BR SSHUB_CONFIG_DIR ).
.TP
.I [sync]
section of
.IR config.toml :
.B auto_sync_on_start
(default true) runs one sync round with every device that has a known host when
the TUI starts;
.B unattended
(default false) marks a device nobody sits at, such as a Hub: it enrolls as an unattended device, never votes, and no
checkpoint or Red change waits for it. The ring is told when the device enrolls, so an already enrolled Hub must
re-enroll to change it;
.B [sync.notify]
(off until
.B command
is set) mails recovery announcements and third-factor codes from
.BR "sshub sync\-serve" :
.B command
is split into arguments without a shell and must read the recipients from the
headers and pass
.BR \-i ,
for example
.BR "/usr/sbin/sendmail \-t \-i" ;
.B to
and
.B from
fill the headers. SMTP credentials belong to that command, never to this file.
.TP
.I sync/ (in a profile)
.I device.key
(this device's identity, encrypted) and
.I peers.json
(which ssh host reaches which device): owner-only, per profile, never copied by
profile transfers.
.TP
.I ~/.ssh/config
Read-only source of ssh_config hosts merged into the launcher. A profile can
point elsewhere with
.I [ssh] config_path
in its
.IR config.toml ;
.B SSHUB_SSH_CONFIG
overrides both.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ssh (1),
.BR ssh_config (5),
.BR sftp (1),
.BR ssh\-add (1)
.PP
